WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.280
This is the Earth, our planet. Home to millions of different species. But only one species

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:27.180
dominates everything. Human beings. There are nearly 7 billion of us living on the Earth.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:33.840
The human population is increasing by more than two people every second. 200,000 people

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:43.280
every day. Nearly 80 million people every year. Each additional life needs food, energy,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:55.480
water, shelter, and hopefully a whole lot more.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.880
Today we're living in an era in which the biggest threat to human wellbeing, to other

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:09.200
species and to the Earth as we know it, might well be ourselves. The issue of population

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.600
size is always controversial because it touches on the most personal decisions we make. But

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.240
we ignore it at our peril.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.440
There's absolutely no doubt at all that the world's population will continue to grow.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.800
The only question is by how much.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.680
More than a billion people on the planet already lack access to safe, clean drinking water.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.640
And we know things are going to get more difficult as the population continues to grow.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:44.000
We need to double the amount of food that we have available to us as soon as possible.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.240
Such a scale of change will leave no one untouched.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.480
Keep in mind that when the Titanic sank, the first class cabins went to the bottom just

00:01:52.480 --> 00:02:03.560
as quickly as the steerage.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:10.760
I was born into a world of just under two billion people. Today there are nearly seven

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:17.000
billion of us. Whenever I hear those numbers, I can honestly say I find it incredible. Triple

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.600
the number of human beings in what seems like the blink of an eye. And the world transformed

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:27.960
utterly.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:34.640
Human population density is a factor in every environmental problem I've ever encountered.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:43.200
From urban sprawl to urban overcrowding. Disappearing tropical forests to ugly sinks of plastic

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:51.440
waste. And now the relentless increase of atmospheric pollution.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.960
I've spent much of the last 50 years seeking wilderness, filming animals in their natural

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.760
habitat and to some extent avoiding humans.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:13.760
But over the years, true wilderness has become harder to find.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.400
I can't pretend that I got involved with filming a natural world 50 years ago because I had

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:26.680
some great banner to carry about conservation. Not at all. I have always had a huge pleasure

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:39.320
in just watching the natural world and seeing what happens.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:46.520
I made those films because it was a hugely enjoyable thing to do. But as I went on making

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:52.160
them, it became more and more apparent that the creatures which were giving me so much

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.480
joy were under threat.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:04.520
The fun is in delighting in the animals. But if you do that, you owe them something. And

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:12.680
so you have an obligation to speak out and do what you can to help protect them.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:19.860
I support a group called the Optimum Population Trust, which campaigns to reduce birth rates.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.360
Because I think if we keep on growing, we're not only going to damage nature, but we're

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.440
likely to see more and more inequality and human suffering.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.080
In this program, I want to see how population growth will affect our ability to obtain our

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:42.080
most basic needs, water, food and energy, and to see if it's possible to answer the

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:49.840
question, how many people can live on planet Earth?

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.880
Human beings are good at many things. But thinking about our species as a whole is not

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.880
one of our strong points.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.640
I don't even think I could tell you how many people live in this country.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:03.640
A Google.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.760
Yeah, I would say a Google.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.560
I know India's population is 1.1 billion, but I don't know the population of the world.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.560
I'd say 6 billion off the top of my head.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.360
I've got no idea how many people live on the planet. No idea.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.080
Luckily, the size of the human population is studied very closely.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.760
By and large, every human birth and death throughout the world has been recorded for

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:36.320
the last 60 years.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:47.720
The data is kept here in New York City at the United Nations.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:56.160
Hania Zlotnik, head of the UN Population Division, is in charge of those precious numbers.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.400
This was the old type of working. When I arrived here at this United Nations, I worked with

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:07.560
these types of files. You see, they're very well organized, but they look old.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:14.800
Now we do it via computer and somehow it's not the same thing as feeling the data.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.440
I am a numbers person, yes, definitely.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:24.200
My mission is to be the beam counter. That means we are the thermometer telling you,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.240
well, the planet is getting hotter, cold in terms of population change.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:35.080
The UN do much more than just keep records. They make projections into the future, and

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.760
their figures are staggering.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.680
The human population is still growing. One expects that at the very least it's likely

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:50.320
to add about 2.3 billion people by the middle of the century. We have 6.8 billion today.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.920
We're expecting to get the seventh billion in the next three, four years. And then we're

00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:02.040
expecting that by mid-century we will have something like nine billion.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:08.560
In the next 40 years, the Earth will need to accommodate nearly three billion more people.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.760
That's more than the current population of the whole of Europe, the whole of Africa,

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:17.120
North and South America combined.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.080
How can we be so sure of this prediction? Well, we know that there are more than a billion

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.280
teenagers alive today, and most of those teenagers will have children of their own and live long

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:33.280
enough to become grandparents. And that's all that needs to happen for there to be nine

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.240
billion humans alive in 2050.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:43.760
It's not people having huge families. It's just a lot of people doing what humans naturally

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.360
do.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.520
We also have a good idea of where these additional people will live. There are likely to be 10

00:07:54.520 --> 00:08:01.360
million more people in Britain, 100 million more in the United States of America. India

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.560
will overtake China to become the most populous country in the world. The population of some

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:15.600
countries will shrink – Japan, Russia, Germany and much of Eastern Europe.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.880
The places that will experience the most rapid growth are also the least developed countries

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:28.760
in the world. Afghanistan's population will double. Most of sub-Saharan Africa will double.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:35.160
Niger's population is predicted to more than triple. I think everyone living through the

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:43.560
next 50 years is going to be affected by these demographic changes wherever they are.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:48.960
For most of human existence, our population size was kept in check by nature, just as

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.760
it is for other animals. If there's plenty of water, food and materials for shelter,

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:02.240
a population will thrive. But when disease, famine or drought strike, life can be cut

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.840
short.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:14.560
The history of humanity is one of overcoming these environmental limits, but it took us

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:24.480
a very long time to achieve.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:32.320
On the horizontal axis here we have time over the last 10,000 years. On the vertical axis

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:40.120
here we have the size of the human population in billions of people. Over the last 10,000

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:46.040
years in general there's been very little change. It's a very boring picture. But

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:52.440
from about the year 1800 onwards you have a major increase, a very large increase in

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:58.160
the world's population from about 1 billion up to 7 billion today.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:04.480
Basically what this increase in population represents is control of death rates. Death

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:14.400
rates have been reduced because infectious diseases, cholera, smallpox, malaria, measles,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.720
those sorts of things have been massively reduced. On average for almost all of human

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:25.360
history a man and a woman were only survived into adulthood by two of their children. And

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:30.160
basically that's why the world's population didn't increase.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:37.040
Extending life by controlling disease is perhaps one of the greatest achievements of humanity.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:44.960
I was born in a world of 2.5 billion and I'm seeing it almost triple in my lifetime. And

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:51.560
life has not gotten worse. In fact for most of the population of the world life has gotten

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.760
better in these 50 years.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:03.280
Living healthily and long has consequences. Population growth.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.960
Just as the human population was starting its unprecedented growth spurt in the late

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:15.960
18th century, this was published. It's a first edition of an essay on population by the English

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:22.640
clergyman Thomas Malthus. Malthus made a very simple observation about the relationship

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:31.680
between humans and the sources and used it to look into the future. He pointed out that

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:39.280
the power of population is indefinitely greater than the power in the earth to produce subsistence

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:47.160
for man. Food production can't increase as rapidly as human reproduction. Demand will

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:53.600
eventually outstrip supply. Malthus goes on to say, if we don't control human reproduction

00:11:53.600 --> 00:12:01.400
voluntarily, life could end in misery, which earned him a reputation as a bit of a pessimist.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:07.320
But Malthus' principle remains true. The productive capacity of the earth has physical

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:19.600
limits and those limits will ultimately determine how many human beings it can support.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:26.280
To help answer that question, we need to have an idea of what human beings need. And the

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:31.360
people who calculate this more precisely than most are the people who are more interested

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:40.080
in leaving the planet and staying on it. Astronauts. One of the people in charge of the well-being

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:48.360
of astronauts on the International Space Station is Doug Hamilton. NASA, we calculate and simulate

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:52.360
everything. If you're going to plan a rocket launch, you have to know how much food and

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:59.480
water and equipment you need to bring into space. As well as working out how much space

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:04.560
the astronauts need, Doug and his team have to calculate their daily requirements for

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:13.680
food, water and breathable air. They typically need about 820 grams of oxygen, which is just

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:21.320
a really large, large balloon, really. We need about 4,000 to 5,000 calories of food,

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:28.440
which is about 820 grams dry. And then we need about 3.52 liters of water, of which

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:33.480
2.5 liters is just consumed daily. We want them to drink a lot of water, it's very good

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.960
for them. And then we urinate out and then we put that into our processing system and

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:44.280
we make it into drinkable water. So you might be drinking the same water molecule hundreds

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:52.040
and hundreds of times on the space station because we recycle. NASA's calculations are

00:13:52.040 --> 00:14:01.720
tailored for space. But they're the same ingredients each and every one of us needs. When you see

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:08.760
how hard it is to reproduce what Mother Nature does every day for all of us, you begin to

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:17.480
really appreciate the world that you have. Whatever our technological achievements, we're

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:24.840
still utterly reliant on the natural systems of the Earth for our very survival.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:31.360
By and large, the planet has provided for the human race so far. As the population has

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:35.520
increased, people through agriculture and industry have exploited those resources ever

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:42.080
more effectively. But increasingly we're seeing signs of strain. We're reaching the limits

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:47.680
of our environment. Perhaps most alarmingly with that fundamental ingredient for life,

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:58.720
water. We call our Earth the Blue Planet because about 70% of the Earth's surface is covered

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:07.800
in water. But most of that is sea. Just 2.5% is fresh water. And of that tiny fraction,

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:14.720
just 1% is available for human use. The rest is locked up in mountain glaciers and the

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:24.320
Earth's polar ice caps. But there's another fact we need to understand about water.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:28.000
Well there's no more water on the planet than there was when life first appeared on

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.280
Earth. It changes its distribution. There's more water in different parts of the world

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:36.260
than there were hundreds or thousands of years ago. But it's still exactly the same amount

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:45.760
of water that's been here always. We appropriate over half of all the available

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:54.400
fresh water in the world to serve our needs. To transform deserts into fields. To generate

00:15:54.400 --> 00:16:01.520
energy from rivers. And to build cities in some of the most arid regions on the planet.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:08.560
But despite our ingenuity, there are many who struggle to get enough of this basic resource.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.440
More than a billion people on the planet already lack access to safe, clean drinking water.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.800
And we know things are going to get more difficult as the population continues to grow. Within

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.720
the next 20 years, as much as half of the world's population will live in areas of

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:26.100
water stress. Chronic water shortages are often the result

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:33.220
of poor infrastructure, politics, poverty, or simply living in an arid part of the world.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:46.560
But increasingly the pressures of population are to blame.

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:53.960
Mexico City is ranked as the 8th richest city in the world. Ahead of Moscow, Hong Kong and

00:16:53.960 --> 00:17:04.040
Washington DC. It also benefits from heavy annual rainfall.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:09.840
But its water system is buckling under the pressure of supplying water to its 20 million

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:20.080
inhabitants. And every day at least a million people are affected by the shortages.

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:28.320
Enrique Vázquez is a water truck driver for the government. And the number of people relying

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:36.880
on this emergency service is growing daily. Today he's heading for a poor district in

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:48.600
the city's southwest, where he's a regular visitor.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:54.020
At some time in the future, waters are going to be fought over water, not oil. But people

00:17:54.020 --> 00:18:04.800
don't seem to understand. Instead of conserving it, we just waste it.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:10.520
The problem is a combination of leaks in the system and backup reservoirs that are running

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:16.800
dry. The city authorities predict that these reservoirs may be completely emptied within

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:21.800
a matter of months.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:34.060
Look, the tap's on, but there's no water coming out.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:42.800
The people living here have had to adapt their lifestyles to an erratic water supply.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:48.000
We only have half a bucket of water to wash ourselves with, and we can't flush the toilet

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:55.440
until two or three people have used it.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:02.800
Unfortunately, I think there's going to be water shortages all over the world, not just

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:10.520
in Mexico City. I think everyone needs to take water more seriously. The few people

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:15.080
who have water should conserve it better, or there'll come a time when the shortages

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:21.480
are global and there's little left for anyone.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:27.200
In Mexico City, shops which sell water to meet people's daily needs are becoming ever

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:33.000
more common. But the water we use at home is only a fraction of the water we actually

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:41.280
consume, and that's because we use colossal quantities in industry and agriculture.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:45.680
We may know where the water out of our tap comes from, but we very seldom know where

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:50.840
the water that went into our can of cola or into the shirt that we're wearing on our

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:56.280
back, where those goods were produced and how much water it required, and what the consequences

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:00.760
were for the natural systems in those areas and for the local communities that are dependent

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:05.560
upon that same water. So, for example, the cup of coffee that you may have in the morning

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:12.040
requires on the order of 120 litres just to produce the coffee and bring it to your table.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:20.920
A can of beer, 150 litres. A hamburger, 8,000 litres of water. To produce enough water to

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:27.920
grow the cotton in my shirt is 3,000 litres as well.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:33.160
The impact of human demands on the world's freshwater systems are so massive they can

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:43.520
be seen from space. The Aral Sea, a freshwater lake in Central Asia, once covered 65,000

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:50.840
square kilometres. In the last 40 years, it has lost 90% of its water. The rivers that

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:57.760
are fed, diverted to irrigate cotton. Lake Chad on the southern edge of the Sahara has

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:03.640
also been drained to a tenth of its former size by drought and overuse, yet 30 million

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:12.440
people depend on it. It is possible to distill fresh water from the sea, and in the last

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:21.880
20 years, more and more countries have turned to desalination. But with current technology,

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:26.920
desalination plants are often extremely expensive, use an enormous amount of energy and their

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:35.520
by-products can be damaging to our seas. With groundwater levels declining across the world

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:41.240
from North Africa to China, pollution of rivers and wetlands on the increase, and already

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:47.680
today more than 1.2 billion people living with water scarcity, our prospects for providing

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:54.280
water to nearly 3 billion more people do not look good. But in many ways, supplying water

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:59.240
to people is the least of our worries. As we've seen, the lion's share of the water

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:04.880
we use goes into agriculture, and that means any water shortages we face in the future

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:14.080
will affect our ability to provide that other staple of life, food. When it comes to the

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:21.280
world's food supply, some of the most accurate information comes from space. Geographer Molly

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:30.360
Brown monitors food production on Earth using data from NASA's satellites. This is an ecosystem

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:35.120
in Thailand where they do rice agriculture, and it's extraordinarily productive and in

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:42.600
one of the most highly productive agricultural regions. Now she's beginning to see global

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:50.040
agriculture hit a natural limit. One of the things that all these different landscapes

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:55.720
really show us is how we are using almost all the land that's available to us that's

00:22:55.720 --> 00:23:02.520
really highly productive, that has great agricultural potential, and so that we know that there

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:07.160
isn't a lot of extra land. I mean, we are using 30 or 40 percent of the entire land

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:14.700
surface. As the world's population increases, the urgency with which we're going to have

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.680
to increase the amount of food we produce will increase, and so we need to double the

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:26.320
amount of food that we have available to us as soon as possible. How we're going to do

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:32.080
that is through raising productivity, because there's really no more land with which to

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:43.440
expand to. A doubling of productivity sounds ambitious, but we've done even better than

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:49.400
that in the past. In the 20th century, the industrialized nations managed to triple their

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:55.720
farming yields with the invention of synthetic fertilizers and then by the introduction of

00:23:55.720 --> 00:24:03.820
mechanized processes. The less developed parts of the world continued using traditional farming

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:12.840
methods into the 1960s until an Iowan farmer decided to do something about it. Norman Borlaug,

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:19.040
who died this year, aged 95, is credited with saving millions of lives in what's become

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:31.520
known as the Green Revolution. So he was a very unpretentious man, and you can see from

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:39.520
his office, small but very functional, and he had some of his awards on the wall, but

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:44.760
also in particular, I always thought this picture, which he kept on the wall, was quite

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:51.680
typical of the kind of person he was, his interactions with the next generation of scientists

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:57.480
around the world, and his enthusiasm for getting out into the field and showing people what

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:03.720
could be done with the science and improving agricultural productivity. Borlaug developed

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:09.760
high-yielding, disease-resistant crops and taught Indian and Mexican farmers how to get

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:20.200
the most out of them with modern farming methods. The astonishing five-fold increases in yield

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:28.640
that they achieved allowed many countries to become self-sufficient in food. In 1970,

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:36.480
Borlaug received the Nobel Peace Prize for his work in alleviating world hunger. He was

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:41.120
able to get his wheat, his new varieties, delivered to India, and within a few years

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:46.080
it was really astounding. He showed me pictures of the mounds of wheat, the surplus that had

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:52.520
been produced within a few years of introducing these new varieties. And in fact, that's the

00:25:52.520 --> 00:26:01.360
seminal event, that's the Green Revolution. Thanks in part to Borlaug, much of the world

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:12.280
is now fed, but globally we're beginning to see a levelling off of agricultural yields.

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:25.240
This is leading to a worrying new trend. To maintain their own food supplies, some of

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:30.600
the richest and most powerful countries in the world are acquiring large tracts of land

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:46.080
from some of the very poorest. Olivier de Scuta is a human rights lawyer who's been monitoring

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:52.560
these land deals for the United Nations. Arab land suitable for cultivation is becoming

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:58.120
a scarce commodity and countries find it more and more difficult to produce enough food

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:04.480
to feed the population. So they are now scrambling in a global competition to achieve food security

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:12.180
by buying land abroad. International corporations and increasingly governments are leasing some

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:18.880
of the last remaining areas of undeveloped farmland in the world. Their aim is to introduce

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:25.440
intensive farming methods and export the food back to their home countries. Well, the problem

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:30.720
is that in most cases these deals are not sufficiently well monitored, they are not

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:38.740
transparent and we are not certain that local communities will benefit from these investments.

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:46.000
These deals are often controversial and shrouded in secrecy, but according to local media reports,

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:52.440
Chinese investors are negotiating land deals throughout Africa as well as with Kazakhstan,

00:27:52.440 --> 00:28:00.280
Mexico and Brazil. Saudi Arabian firms have leased farmland in Sudan and several British

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:06.840
investment funds are reported to be raising capital to buy farmland in Angola, Malawi

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:12.740
and Ukraine. Most of the target countries for foreign investors are in Africa, some

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:19.460
of which already struggle to feed their own people. Well we see paradoxical situations

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:24.640
such as foreign investors producing food in Ethiopia, shipping this food back to the home

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:28.920
country or selling it on the international markets, when Ethiopia is still a country

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:33.520
which is heavily dependent on international food aid. So this is a country which is at

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:39.280
the same time producing food for export markets and depending on international aid in order

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:46.160
to feed this population. The future is going to be particularly challenging for the countries

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:51.720
of sub-Saharan Africa. With many of their populations projected to double, there's

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:59.600
going to be increasing pressure for a limited supply of land. There are few nations as acutely

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:06.680
aware of how destabilising these kinds of pressures can be as Rwanda. Our land is not

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:15.400
growing and yet our population is. We estimate that it will be double in 26 years. So in

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:26.880
26 years it probably will be 20 million. Rwandans consider land a vital resource but they also

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:35.000
see it as a resource for primarily their own use, for their own security, for their own

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:45.320
food security. Martin Seturinka grows bananas and maize on three acres of farmland.

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:55.160
Like 80% of Rwandans, his family subsists on what they can grow. Land is an issue all

00:29:55.160 --> 00:30:01.160
over Rwanda. There isn't enough land to go around and people find it hard to grow enough

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:08.880
food to survive. In Rwanda, children inherit land from their parents but in a country where

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:14.820
the average family has more than five children, that can only mean one thing – smaller parcels

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:27.000
of land to live off. I don't know what will happen to my children or how they will cope.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:35.400
I honestly don't. It's already impossible for me to provide enough food for them. Only

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:47.600
God knows. Martin is father to 15 children but they aren't all his own. Five of them

00:30:47.600 --> 00:31:01.400
are adopted, orphans whose parents were brutally murdered in Rwanda's devastating genocide.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:08.280
In 1994, the two major tribes in Rwanda, the Hutus and the Tutsis, embarked on a mutual

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:20.340
slaughter that left almost a million dead in just three months. Amongst the many causes

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:29.240
of that conflict, competition for scarce resources was an added pressure.

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:35.520
Slaughter became a mobilising tool. The poor, unemployed youth, some of them were encouraged

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:44.120
to kill their neighbours with the hope that they would either inherit their piece of land

00:31:44.120 --> 00:32:00.240
or their house or their livestock. If we can't grow the economy fast enough to meet this

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:06.000
growth and can't slow it down, then there will be increased competition for resources

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:13.680
which are finite so our forests are likely to go, our swamps will be overused and therefore

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:21.200
this will also have an effect on the climate, climatic changes which will further exacerbate

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:26.240
the negative effects of this growth so it's a bit of a vicious cycle and we must find

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:34.200
a way of breaking it.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:43.160
In Rwanda, the government can already foresee the impact population growth is likely to

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:49.440
have on their immediate environment. Across the world, population growth is likely to

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:55.720
take an even greater toll because of our ever increasing demands for a resource we've come

00:32:55.720 --> 00:33:05.240
to depend on but which may be causing us the biggest damage of all.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:10.040
Of all the resources that humans have harnessed from the earth, the one that has transformed

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:15.960
everything is energy. Fossil fuels are the remains of plants and animals that lived perhaps

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:23.040
350 million years ago and later became buried in the earth's crust. With the technologies

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:28.680
of the industrial age, we liberated this energy and used it to get more from nature than had

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:35.640
ever been possible before. Our favourite fossil fuel is oil. Our demand for it increases every

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:44.320
year. Today we use 85 million barrels a day. Oil provides the fertiliser, pesticide and

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:51.920
mechanisation that has allowed us so far to produce enough food for our expanding population.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:34:02.520
But just as we're realising how much we depend on it, it's getting harder to find.

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:09.400
Houston, Texas. One of the richest places in the world thanks to its vast reserves of

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:21.080
oil and gas. Danny Davis is an independent oil producer.

00:34:21.080 --> 00:34:31.040
Alright, this is our office, kind of our base of operations and what we do. Our little company

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:36.800
sign which we're very proud of. Danny has been drilling oil in Texas since the early

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:41.200
1980s. This is a collection of oil, jars of oil on different wells that we've found over

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:45.480
the years, I guess over the last 15 years. And one of them that I kind of like the most

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:50.680
is this one right here. This was discovered about 30 minutes out of Houston on the Brookshire

00:34:50.680 --> 00:34:57.040
Dome. This well came under a thousand barrels of oil a day from 2500 feet. It's high gravity

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:00.800
sweet crude, it smells great. When this comes out of the ground and it's fresh, you can

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:05.640
put it on your salad, the oil and vinegar is good stuff. But this is why we do it, this

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:12.360
is what it's all about. So it's just an exciting business. There's a fortune to be made treating

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:19.360
this reservoir in these wells. These days oil in Texas is getting harder to find. Dan

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:25.120
is looking much further afield to Alaska. He's been granted a rare licence from the

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:32.120
government to drill offshore. But before he can get started, Danny needs to raise millions

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:39.000
of dollars of investment. Let me ask you a question. How many years have you been doing

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:46.000
this? About 40 or 50? If his plans are successful, the figures are truly staggering. You look

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:49.600
at a billion barrels of oil and an oil of 70 dollars a barrel and you've got two billion

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:56.600
barrels gross numbers, 200 billion dollars probably, I don't know. I couldn't predict

00:35:56.600 --> 00:36:00.640
that. But you can only go with what the value is today, you don't know what it's going

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:05.640
to be tomorrow. Yeah, I'll call him and let him know. Thanks for everything. Alright guys,

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:12.640
we'll see you all soon. Danny won't be short of customers for his oil because energy demand

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:18.640
is predicted to increase by 40 percent over the next two decades. The Alaskan fields may

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:24.320
make him a very wealthy man. But the fossil fuels that have helped to bring great wealth

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:30.280
to many nations as well as individuals are proving to be a double edged sword and not

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:36.280
just because of their contribution to climate change. What cheap energy has allowed us to

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:43.280
do fundamentally is to appropriate the earth's natural systems to serve our needs without

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:48.280
paying too much attention to the long term effects on the environment and other species.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:55.280
It seems we're just beginning to realise the full impact that our industrialisation

00:36:55.280 --> 00:37:02.280
is having upon the natural world. In the oceans we've depleted fish stocks massively. Ten

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:09.280
percent of the world's coral reefs are estimated to be degraded beyond recovery. A third of

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:17.400
the world's amphibians, a fifth of all mammals and 70 percent of all plants are currently

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:23.640
under threat of extinction. When it comes to conserving our natural world, there are

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:29.320
two arguments to contend with. On the one hand, there's a sense of our moral obligation

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:35.240
as the most intelligent species on the planet to protect the marvellous variety of species

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:41.840
that have evolved alongside us. On the other, there's self-interest. The more we damage

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:47.640
the environment, the more we threaten our own survival. Perhaps self-interest is the

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:53.900
more powerful argument because how we treat our environment certainly determines how many

00:37:53.900 --> 00:38:00.900
people the Earth can sustain. There's a concept in ecology called carrying capacity. It's

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:08.960
a calculation of how large a population any given environment can support. William Rees

00:38:08.960 --> 00:38:15.960
is a human ecologist who's taken the concept and applied it to ourselves and our environment,

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:34.720
the Earth. The simple fact of the matter is that the Earth can accommodate so much consumption

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:40.200
and you might have 10 billion people at one level of living and a billion at a more comfortable

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:45.200
level of living. So carrying capacity is a very flexible idea. You simply divide the

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:48.840
total productivity of the Earth by the number of people and that gives you some idea of

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:55.840
how many people the Earth can support. Rees has estimated what he calls the productive

00:38:55.840 --> 00:39:02.840
bio-capacity of the Earth. This is made up of all the food, water and energy produced

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:10.440
across the world each year and measured in units called global hectares. He's worked

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:17.440
out that if we were to share the Earth's productive bio-capacity fairly, there'd be two global

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:24.440
hectares each. But the reality tells a very different story. The Earth's bio-capacity

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:31.440
is a very different story. According to Rees' data, most of Africa use little more than

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:38.440
half their share of the Earth's productive capacity. The average Indian uses less than

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:45.440
half. The Chinese use their fair allocation of two hectares each. But Europeans use much

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:53.280
more with the British on average using over five global hectares. And the average American

00:39:53.280 --> 00:40:00.280
using more than four times their fair share.

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:08.680
So how many people can the Earth sustain? Well, according to these calculations, if

00:40:08.680 --> 00:40:14.280
all humans consumed the same as the average Indian does today, the Earth could sustain

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:21.280
as many as 15 billion people. If we consumed as little as the average Rwandan, this would

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:28.280
go up to 18 billion. But our planet can only sustain two and a half billion people living

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:33.680
as we do in Britain and only one and a half billion living in the lifestyle of those in

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:40.680
the United States. But the picture may be even worse than this. These figures are based

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:48.240
on rates of consumption that many think are already unsustainable.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:53.920
There's plenty of evidence right now that we are already in a state of what we call

00:40:53.920 --> 00:41:00.120
overshoot. That each year the human population at current average levels of consumption,

00:41:00.120 --> 00:41:04.560
which most of us in Europe and North America would consider to be inadequate, is already

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:09.960
exceeding the productive capacity of the planet. Not only in terms of its ability to produce,

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:16.000
but also in terms of its capacity to assimilate our wastes.

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:22.440
This believes that today's population requires the equivalent of one and a half Earths to

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:29.160
support our current way of life. We're simply living beyond the means of our environment

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:32.000
to sustain us.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:36.400
To have a state of sustainability where we remain within the productive capacity of the

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:42.640
planet means that people in industrialized countries are going to have to give up consumption

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:48.520
of a great deal in order to create the ecological space for needed growth in the third world.

00:41:48.520 --> 00:41:52.680
If we don't make those kinds of compromises, then we're going to continue to erode the

00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:59.440
resource base of the planet to the point where we all suffer.

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:05.480
As I see it, humanity needs to reduce its impact on the Earth urgently. And there are

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:13.320
three ways to achieve this. We can stop consuming so many resources, we can change our technology

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:20.280
and we can reduce the growth of our population. We probably need to do all three.

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:25.840
For most people, the idea of someone else telling them how many children they should

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:32.320
have is simply unacceptable. So when governments attempt to do exactly that, it always causes

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:41.320
controversy. In 1979, the Chinese government introduced its infamous one child policy,

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:55.600
changing family life in China forever.

00:42:55.600 --> 00:43:01.360
Families were encouraged to have fewer children. Those that didn't were fined. The policy

00:43:01.360 --> 00:43:10.120
was a direct response to the preceding decades of famine and starvation.

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:16.400
It's still in place today. According to official figures, without the one child policy, there'd

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:24.560
be 400 million more people in China. That's more than the entire population of the USA.

00:43:24.560 --> 00:43:29.560
It's unlikely that other governments could undertake such an extreme path without major

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:35.120
civil opposition.

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:41.880
In the 1970s, the Indian government also sought to bring down its birth rate. To start with,

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:47.400
it took a less aggressive path, setting up festivals around the country where vasectomies

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:51.200
were offered in return for small incentives.

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:57.920
In those days, in those festivals, they have done in a week something like 80,000 sterilization.

00:43:57.920 --> 00:44:02.960
The incentive was some cash, some money, nothing much.

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:08.000
The problem was the festivals were attracting the wrong customers, people who already had

00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:10.140
large families.

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:17.360
That is the weakness of incentivization. They could not attract couples with two children.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:23.320
They attract couples with five children, six children. It is like you are closing the door

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:26.240
after the horse is gone.

00:44:26.240 --> 00:44:33.800
But in some areas, politicians took the sterilization drive a step too far.

00:44:33.800 --> 00:44:43.040
In 1977, when Indira Gandhi was introduced to the emergency program, what they did, the

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:50.080
punishment for every crime in those days was sterilization. For example, if a person travels

00:44:50.080 --> 00:45:00.040
in the train, he has no tickets. What is the punishment? He was taken for sterilization.

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:11.280
In 1977 alone, around eight million people were sterilized. And the public outcry was

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:17.520
so great that it helped to bring down the government. Hopefully, these kinds of coercive

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:22.520
policies are a thing of the past, because we are beginning to realize that birth rates

00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:34.560
fall provided the conditions are right.

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:41.760
In the southwest of India lies the long, narrow coastal state of Kerala. Most of its 32 million

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:47.480
inhabitants live off the land and the ocean, a rich tropical ecosystem watered by two months

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:59.080
and a year. It is also one of India's most crowded states. But the population is stable

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:09.920
because nearly everybody has small families.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:13.560
How many of you have only one child in the house? Raise your hands. Only one. You are

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:20.600
the only one in the house. Only one. Only one. I think today almost 30 to 40 percent

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:26.840
couples in Kerala have just one child. How many of you have two in the house? Two. Two

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:34.040
in the house. Today, an average Kerala woman produces only 1.5 children. How many of you?

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:39.880
Three in the house? Three, three? Three? No problem. Brother or sister? Two brothers.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:44.920
They wanted a girl. That's why they had three. Otherwise, no. You will rarely see a couple

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:56.680
with no three children. Very rarely.

00:46:56.680 --> 00:47:02.320
At the root of it all is education. Thanks to a long tradition of compulsory schooling

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:11.720
for boys and girls, Kerala has one of the highest literacy rates in the world. Even

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:24.840
two young children are coming to school. They are carrying bags bigger than them.

00:47:24.840 --> 00:47:34.680
While women are well educated, they tend to choose to have smaller families.

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:41.000
When all girls go to school, automatically they will marry very late. For example, today

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:48.040
in Kerala, average women marry at the age of 28. Whereas the state like Uttar Pradesh,

00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:56.280
Rajasthan, Bighar, the girl marrys at the age of 18. So, at 28, these women in the Uttar

00:47:56.280 --> 00:48:01.920
Pradesh and Bighar have already four children where Kerala girl is not married. How many

00:48:01.920 --> 00:48:13.400
children you want to have? One. One.

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:19.800
What Kerala shows is that you don't need aggressive policies or government incentives for birth

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:21.800
rates to fall.

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:26.400
Everywhere in the world where women have access to education and have the freedom to run their

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:31.840
own lives, on the whole, they and their partners have been choosing to have smaller families

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:33.440
than their parents did.

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:39.340
But reducing birth rates is very difficult to achieve without a simple piece of medical

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:43.240
technology, contraception.

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:49.640
You can think of modern contraception as a crucial technology for the sustainability

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:55.880
of the planet because it's the element that has allowed the populations of many developing

00:48:55.880 --> 00:49:05.680
countries to reduce their fertility as rapidly as they have done it.

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:11.240
Despite a recent history that makes population a particularly delicate subject in Rwanda,

00:49:11.240 --> 00:49:17.040
the government here is one of the few in Africa to have made universal access to contraception

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:21.800
a national priority in recent years.

00:49:21.800 --> 00:49:27.740
Console Mukanyawaya is one of hundreds of family planning officers who give contraceptive

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:32.200
advice to local communities.

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:39.600
Since the year 2000, family planning education has been provided for everyone in the country.

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:47.840
Rwandans understand that while it's wonderful to have children, you've got to be able to

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:50.880
look after them as well as you can.

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:55.840
We try to get people who use contraception to teach their neighbours so they can see

00:49:55.840 --> 00:50:02.600
for themselves the advantages of having fewer children.

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:07.400
Since it has become freely available, the uptake for contraception has been huge in

00:50:07.400 --> 00:50:16.000
Rwanda, with many women opting for injections or even five-year hormone implants.

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:20.680
While Rwanda is addressing its population growth, it's estimated that a quarter of

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:26.600
married women in sub-Saharan Africa still don't have any access to contraception, and

00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:32.520
across the world, over 80 million births are unplanned.

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:37.280
In my view, it's a basic human right that everyone should have access to contraception.

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:44.280
All the evidence is that people take advantage of this once they have the possibility, and

00:50:44.280 --> 00:50:46.320
they reduce their fertility.

00:50:46.320 --> 00:50:51.240
If that happens, then amongst other things, the world's population growth will eventually

00:50:51.240 --> 00:51:03.880
level out at a lower rather than a higher number, and that's a good thing.

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:09.200
When it comes to other ways of reducing human impact on the earth, there are a few glimmers

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:11.560
of hope emerging.

00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:16.000
Governments across the world are beginning to recognise that the life support services

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:21.200
provided by our ecosystems are in need of repair, and they're doing something about

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:26.520
it.

00:51:26.520 --> 00:51:31.480
Often it takes individuals with vision to lead the process of change.

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:40.280
Malente Souza is an urban planner and a committed environmentalist with a lot of responsibility.

00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:45.000
The government of Mexico City have employed him to find a sustainable solution to their

